-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  std_hookio_linux.pcpp - std compliance: the HookIO unit, the registry and the open
  family

  `import HookIO;` RegisterIO / RegisterIOSource give a prefix `io://<n>`;
  C's fopen on a path under it opens the registered stream and returns a
  HookIO handle (FileIO gives the IO behind it), any other path goes to the
  C runtime. Covered over the three kinds of stream: a memory IO, a file IO
  (the program's own file) and a zip IOSource (std_customio_stored.zip, the
  std_customio fixture written by scripts\customio\gen_zips.py). Bytes read
  through a handle's IO are compared with the same bytes read another way
  (the caller's buffer, the C runtime's fread, CreateZipFileIO).

  A prefix behind leading "./" steps (".\" too on Windows, also through
  _wfopen) opens the same stream; behind a folder it is a plain path.

  Then every refusal: an unregistered prefix, prefix 0, a leading zero, no
  number, a missing entry, an entry in a mode the zip does not offer, a path
  the source refuses (ENOENT; EACCES for the mode), w on a registered IO, w+
  and an unknown mode letter (EINVAL); registering nil; unregistering, twice,
  and a path that is not a prefix. Windows also opens through fopen_s and
  _wfopen, Linux through fopen64, each on a virtual path, a missing one and a
  real one. Every stream the program makes is closed at the end.

  The stream family from C: std_hookio_lib (std_hookio_c.c, a static library,
  built first) reads a virtual file and the program's own exe with fseek /
  ftell / rewind / fgetc / ungetc / fread / ferror / feof, reads a virtual
  text file with fgets, and writes a virtual file ("ab") with fwrite / fputc
  / fflush; bytes and sizes are compared with what the program knows. Then
  from PaskalCPP: every seek origin, a seek before the start and past the end
  of a fixed IO (EINVAL), ftell after ungetc, feof only after a short read,
  ferror after a write on a read-only handle and a read on a write-only one
  (EBADF), clearerr, fileno (-1, EBADF), setvbuf, fputc / fputs.

  Past 4 GiB: a CreateIO stream that stores nothing (it reports 5 GiB + 32
  and makes each byte from its place) is read from C with the target's
  64-bit names (Windows _fseeki64 / _ftelli64, Linux fseeko / ftello, both
  fseeko64 / ftello64) from the start, the end and the current place, with
  fgetc / getc there; ftell's long is -1 with EINVAL on Windows (32 bits,
  as ucrt's) and holds the place on Linux, both checked against a real file
  seeked to the same place. The printf family writes one virtual file
  from C (fprintf, vfprintf, Windows __mingw_fprintf) and from the program's
  own C++ (std::fprintf / std::vfprintf). The wide names (fputwc, putwc and
  its real symbol, fputws, fwide), under the "C" locale and a UTF-8 one,
  alone and mixed with the narrow names, unbuffered, line buffered, with
  fseek between writes and appending, write the bytes a real file of the
  same mode gets, every call's value, errno, ferror, fwide and ftell equal
  to the real file's (each target's mode set). fflush(nil) reaches a
  handle (its IO's Flush runs), getc / putc from PaskalCPP, Windows
  _lock_file / _unlock_file do nothing on ours. C's fscanf, then each other
  scan name of the target (Windows __mingw_fscanf, vfscanf, fscanf_s; Linux
  the plain fscanf symbol, vfscanf, the plain vfscanf symbol), reads one
  virtual text file and a real file of the same text: the numbers, the place
  and feof after each scan equal the real file's, also for a scan past more
  whitespace than one stand-in window holds. Linux: glibc's fortified names
  (what C built with -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE calls: __fgets_chk,
  __fgets_unlocked_chk, __fread_chk, __fread_unlocked_chk, __fprintf_chk,
  __vfprintf_chk), called from C by name, read a virtual file and a real one
  of the same bytes (every slot equal, also where the _chk rules differ from
  the plain names: n past the buffer on a short line, n of 0 and 1) and
  write the same text into a virtual file as into a real one.

  Each row prints ok or FAIL with its name; the last is `done <failures>`.

  The Linux half of std_hookio: the same program, the Linux
  expectations.
*******************************************************************************/

module exe std_hookio_linux;

